9 sites, 9 databases. For example, a team called Acme would get the site cognitive-tours-acme and the database ct-acme. The full table is in CLAUDE.md, under "Team sites".

Facts from Claude Nov 3:

Same code everywhere. One push to main deploys all 9. Only the environment variables differ:
TURSO_DATABASE_URL and TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN: which database the site uses;
SEED_ON_EMPTY=false: a team starts with an empty map (app/api/data/route.ts, from 46f4f82);
NEXT_PUBLIC_PROJECT_NAME: the "Project:" label (a0d73f3);
NEXT_PUBLIC_REPO_URL, optional: links file paths to the team's GitHub repo (7db0dd8).
No team code at all. There is no team id, no login and no filtering. A team's data is separate because it's physically in a different database.

Why it works this way (decided Oct 20, when students started writing to their sites):

Turso's own restore only reaches back one day on my plan.
Team maps are student work, so they never go into the git repo.
Rejected:
a job on my Mac: no backups while it's off;
a GitHub Action: nowhere private enough to keep student work;
each site backing itself up: too many secrets to manage;
Turso database dumps: they need credentials, and the restore tools expect the map format.
This note was Drafted with Claude's help, Nov 4.
